<!--
    decbench campaign case novel-concat-subpiece-soup (NOVEL pool: kuna already
    scored best of all decompilers on the panes that surfaced it and the output
    was still not C, so no other decompiler showed us this fix).

    P3 simplification quiescence, cleanup pool.  RuleSubRight (upstream
    ruleaction.cc:7271, registered "subright" in the cleanup pool at
    infra/universalaction.rs) rewrites a truncation that is NOT least
    significant into a shift plus a least significant truncation:

        sub(V,c)  =>  sub(V >> c*8, 0)

    Only the special print half of that rule was ported; the ~45 line rewrite
    tail was a stub returning 0.  So every SUBPIECE with a nonzero byte offset
    survived to the printer, which renders a non zero offset SUBPIECE as the
    raw p-code operator SUBnn.  SUB41 / SUB84 / SUB81 are undeclared
    identifiers (kuna emits no header defining them), so the emitted
    function is not compilable C:

        *a0   = SUB41(a1,3);                          <- put_u32_be
        return SUB84((uint8)a1 * (uint8)a0,4) >> 3;   <- mulhi_shift

    Upstream Ghidra prints the shift form for both, and so does kuna's own
    offset-0 SUBPIECE (a0[3] = (char)a1), which is what proves the printer was
    never the problem.

    Bytes: gcc -m32 -O1 -fno-stack-protector -fno-pic on

        void put_u32_be(u8 *p, u32 v) { p[0] = (v >> 24) & 0xff; ... }
        u32  mulhi_shift(u32 a, u32 b) { return (u32)(((u64)a * b) >> 32) >> 3; }

    put_u32_be covers the plain rewrite (three truncations at byte offsets
    3/2/1, plus the offset-0 one the c==0 bail must leave alone).  mulhi_shift
    covers the lone-descendant lumping: the mull high half is SUBPIECE(prod,4)
    whose only reader is a constant INT_RIGHT, so the rule folds the two into a
    single shift by 32+3 == 0x23.  It is written with a variable multiplier so
    that RuleDivOpt declines and the truncation reaches the printer.

    No option: this is a correctness fix (an undeclared p-code operator in the
    C body is never desired) restoring upstream's own behavior from a port
    stub, so it is a Convergence, not a divergence (see docs/history.md).
    Two-pass anyway, using the rule's existing name in the action pool:
    pass 1 is the default (the fix), pass 2 sets `option togglerule subright
    off` to pin the pre-fix rendering.
-->
